WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.140
An ex-Borg moves to Boston.&nbsp;
A half-Klingon moves behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.520
the camera. A Starfleet captain goes to&nbsp;
prison. Where did the cast of Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.580
Voyager end up once they returned&nbsp;
from the far reaches of the galaxy?

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.540
Kate Mulgrew wasn't the first choice&nbsp;
to play Captain Janeway in Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.060
Voyager. Academy Award-nominee Geneviève Bujold&nbsp;
was famously cast first, but filmed only a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.900
scenes before quitting the show during&nbsp;
the series pilot. Today it's difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.220
to imagine anyone else in the role, though&nbsp;
it's hardly Mulgrew's only iconic TV series.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.560
Following Voyager's conclusion in 2001,&nbsp;
Mulgrew took a few years off from acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.840
returning with a small role in the 2005&nbsp;
film Perception. After a guest appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.180
Law &amp; Order: Special Victims Unit, Mulgrew snagged&nbsp;
a recurring role on The Black Donnellys in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:51.720
and another in the short-lived NBC medical drama&nbsp;
Mercy in 2009. Her return to a main cast, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:57.240
came in the Adult Swim series NTSF:SD:SUV::,&nbsp;
where she played an eye patch-wearing leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.860
of an anti-terrorism task force alongside&nbsp;
future Star Trek star Rebecca Romijn.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.900
Mulgrew found a major career resurgence in&nbsp;
2013, starring in one of Netflix's earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.180
forays into original programming, Orange is&nbsp;
the New Black. In the series she stars as Red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.640
an inmate at a women's prison. The role that would&nbsp;
earn her an Emmy nomination. Mulgrew returned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.500
Star Trek in 2021, voicing Kathryn Janeway&nbsp;
in the animated series Star Trek: Prodigy.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.480
"Captain Janeway?"

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.500
"I prefer Vice Admiral Janeway."

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.680
Sitting in the chair next to Captain&nbsp;
Janeway for seven seasons was Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.540
Beltran as Commander Chakotay, a former member&nbsp;
of the anti-Federation, freedom-fighting Maquis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:41.580
Though Beltran counts his heritage as Latino,&nbsp;
Chakotay was the first Native American regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.500
character in the franchise, but was sadly&nbsp;
under-used. Following Voyager, Beltran's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.000
work on the small screen was mostly limited to&nbsp;
guest appearances, popping up in episodes of CSI:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.160
Miami and Medium in the 2000s, while filling&nbsp;
roles in movies like Fire Serpent and Manticore.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.040
Beltran's first recurring part on TV after Voyager&nbsp;
was in the series Big Love, playing Jerry Flute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.240
another Native American character, who has&nbsp;
plans to construct a casino on a reservation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.640
Over the next decade, Beltran moved away from&nbsp;
acting, with a sparse handful of minor roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.960
He revealed on Twitter that he turned down a&nbsp;
chance to play Chakotay one more time in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.980
revival series Star Trek: Picard, as he was&nbsp;
unhappy with the part they'd written for him.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.620
Nevertheless, Beltran did come back to join Kate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.700
Mulgrew for Prodigy. Voicing Chakotay in&nbsp;
his triumphant return to the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.320
the series sees the character lost in space and&nbsp;
his former captain on a mission to find him.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.860
Actor Tim Russ had already made a&nbsp;
few guest appearances in the Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.020
Trek franchise before joining the main cast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.360
Voyager in 1995. But perhaps his most&nbsp;
notable pre-Trek role was this one.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.680
"What about you guys?"

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.560
"We ain't found s–t!"

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.080
Russ became a fan-favorite as the Vulcan Lt.&nbsp;
Tuvok. After seven seasons playing the stoic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.680
emotionless Tuvok, Russ kept busy&nbsp;
with a variety of different roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.840
mostly guest-starring in popular TV hits.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.500
This included guest spots in&nbsp;
everything from ER and Law &amp; Order:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.980
Criminal Intent to episodes of&nbsp;
Hannah Montana and Without a Trace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.360
and a multi-episode appearance on the hit&nbsp;
soap General Hospital. That same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.620
Russ joined the main cast of the Christina&nbsp;
Applegate comedy Samantha Who? and later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.200
began working in video games, providing voice work&nbsp;
for Wolfenstein II: The New Colossus and others.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.940
Since then, Russ has kept busy with countless&nbsp;
roles in a huge variety of other TV shows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.800
More recently, Russ turned up in an episode of&nbsp;
Seth MacFarlane's Star Trek homage The Orville,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:34.320
and in 2023 voiced Lucius Fox in the animated&nbsp;
film Batman: The Doom That Came to Gotham.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.900
On Voyager, the role of chief engineer was filled&nbsp;
by the half-Klingon/half-human B'Elanna Torres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.760
played by Roxann Dawson. Starting out as a&nbsp;
Maquis rebel, she eventually becomes one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.800
most important members of the crew, as well as a&nbsp;
wife and mother. Following her run on the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.780
Dawson had just a handful of on-screen roles,&nbsp;
which included single episodes of The Closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.280
and Without a Trace. That's because, like&nbsp;
her franchise cohort Jonathan Frakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.580
Dawson moved behind the camera&nbsp;
to become a full-time director.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.080
Getting her start overseeing episodes&nbsp;
of Voyager, Dawson moved on to helm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:12.120
entries of the Star Trek spin-off Enterprise&nbsp;
before broadening to other shows. Since 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.200
Dawson has directed episodes of some of&nbsp;
the biggest hits on TV, including Lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.540
and The O.C. in 2006, eight episodes of&nbsp;
Cold Case, and a trio of Heroes episodes.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:27.000
Among her most notable shows might be&nbsp;
the David Simon HBO series Treme in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.580
Hell on Wheels with future starship captain Anson&nbsp;
Mount and Deep Space Nine engineer Colm Meaney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.740
and modern masterpieces like Bates Motel, The&nbsp;
Americans, and This is Us. Her most recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.060
work saw her return to sci-fi, helming two&nbsp;
episodes of the Apple TV+ series Foundation.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:48.120
Despite perpetually remaining a low-level ensign,&nbsp;
Harry Kim, played by Garrett Wang, often played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.620
a crucial role in defeating many of the enemies&nbsp;
the crew would face in the Delta Quadrant. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:58.020
Voyager left TV, Wang bounced around in smaller&nbsp;
roles. He's continued embracing his role as Ensign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.920
Kim by appearing at many fan conventions,&nbsp;
where he found an entirely new calling.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.080
Beginning in 2010, Kim embarked on a career as&nbsp;
an event moderator, serving as the Master of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.940
Ceremonies at that year's FedCon, a science&nbsp;
fiction convention held in Germany. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.500
he was the Trek Track Director at the celebrated&nbsp;
Dragon Con, held annually in Atlanta, Georgia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.640
Over the course of his new career,&nbsp;
Wang has held moderating duties and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.080
hosted panels and events at major pop culture&nbsp;
conventions in Montreal, Edmonton, Phoenix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:30.180
and Denver. According to Wang, his biggest&nbsp;
role as a moderator came at the Calgary Comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.260
and Entertainment Expo in 2012, where&nbsp;
he interviewed the legendary Stan Lee.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.700
In 2020, Wang joined forces with&nbsp;
co-star Robert Duncan McNeill to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.020
launch "The Delta Flyers," a podcast that&nbsp;
discusses classic episodes of Voyager.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.620
Robert Duncan McNeill guest-starred&nbsp;
in an episode of Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.400
The Next Generation as a hotshot pilot&nbsp;
who broke the rules and wound up booted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.400
from Starfleet. So when producers&nbsp;
developed a similar character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.080
they brought in McNeill to play him, resulting&nbsp;
in brash, cavalier helm officer Tom Paris.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.420
After Voyager ended, McNeill starred in an episode&nbsp;
of The Outer Limits revival and a few more small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.460
roles. However, like Roxann Dawson, McNeill&nbsp;
left acting not long after the series ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.420
to become a director and producer, starting&nbsp;
with four episodes of Star Trek: Enterprise.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.860
Into the 2000s, McNeill helmed&nbsp;
episodes of Dawson's Creek and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.240
One Tree Hill before becoming an executive&nbsp;
producer on the action-comedy series Chuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.340
where he'd direct 21 episodes across its&nbsp;
five seasons. From there, McNeill went on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:30.360
to sit behind the camera for installments&nbsp;
of other shows, like The Orville and Suits.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.220
Since the 2010s, McNeill has served as an&nbsp;
executive producer on further shows that included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.100
The Gifted, the Disney+ reboot of Turner &amp; Hooch,&nbsp;
and the SyFy series Resident Alien. In addition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.960
hosting "The Delta Flyers" podcast with co-star&nbsp;
Garrett Wang, McNeill came back to Star Trek in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:51.060
2022 when he voiced the character of Tom Paris&nbsp;
on the animated comedy Star Trek: Lower Decks.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.340
"This is how we kick ass in the Delta Quadrant!"

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.560
Another actor to appear on Star Trek before taking&nbsp;
a leading role on Voyager, Ethan Phillips played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.960
the quirky alien chef Neelix for all seven seasons&nbsp;
of the show's run. A well-established TV veteran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.880
his roles prior had included dramas like&nbsp;
NYPD Blue and family hits like Doogie Howser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.320
M.D. After his role on Voyager, he went back&nbsp;
to his former career as a character actor.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.220
In the ensuing years, Phillips could&nbsp;
be seen all over the dial and beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.520
with parts in shows like Touched&nbsp;
by an Angel and 8 Simple Rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.180
and even a guest-starring role in&nbsp;
an episode of Enterprise in 2002.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:29.700
Later he did a three-episode run on Boston Legal,&nbsp;
a legal drama from David E. Kelley starring Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:35.280
Trek legend William Shatner and Deep Space Nine&nbsp;
alum René Auberjonois. One of his episodes just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.840
happened to also feature Deep Space Nine's Armin&nbsp;
Shimerman. Some of the biggest shows he's found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.540
work on during the 2010s include Better Call Saul&nbsp;
and a recurring role in Lena Dunham's Girls. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:50.340
also had roles in major movies, showing up in&nbsp;
Inside Llewyn Davis and The Purge: Election Year.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:54.840
Though he hasn't come back to Star Trek,&nbsp;
Phillips did return to sci-fi in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.740
joining the main cast of the&nbsp;
HBO space comedy Avenue 5.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.420
Though she didn't arrive on Voyager until&nbsp;
Season 4, Jeri Ryan arguably became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.440
series' biggest star, joining the cast&nbsp;
as a former Borg drone named Seven of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.000
Nine. It proved to be just what the series&nbsp;
needed and a career-defining role for Ryan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.200
One of the few cast members of Voyager&nbsp;
to parlay her role into bigger success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.700
Ryan immediately joined the David E. Kelley&nbsp;
drama Boston Public after the series ended.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:27.180
In 2006, she secured another starring role in the&nbsp;
James Woods series Shark. Smaller recurring roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.340
came after, including multi-episode stints on Law&nbsp;
&amp; Order: SVU, Two and a Half Men, and Leverage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:37.200
before Ryan returned to a main cast with her&nbsp;
co-starring role in Body of Proof in 2011.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.000
More recurring roles over the years lead right&nbsp;
up to her return to Star Trek: Picard in 2020.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.800
Returning to the role of Seven of Nine, she&nbsp;
supported series lead Patrick Stewart by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.880
appearing in all three seasons, and rumor&nbsp;
has it she may even star in a spin-off.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.520
Joining the crew of Voyager was Kes, a young&nbsp;
alien woman with mild telepathic powers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.620
a nine-year lifespan, played by Jennifer Lien.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, her character never quite seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.180
to fit, and in Season 4 Lien was written out&nbsp;
to make way for Jeri Ryan as Seven of Nine.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.120
Leaving the series in 1997, Lien's career stalled&nbsp;
in front of the camera, though she did play a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:17.640
in American History X alongside Star Trek: Deep&nbsp;
Space Nine star Avery Brooks. However, most of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:22.200
subsequent work came in animation, with voice work&nbsp;
in Superman: The Animated Series, where she played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.880
Inza, the wife of Doctor Fate, and a starring&nbsp;
role as Agent L in Men in Black: The Series.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.760
Lien pretty much left acting shortly after that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.640
She married filmmaker Phil&nbsp;
Hwang and started a family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.260
but has faced personal problems along the way.&nbsp;
While struggling to deal with her mental health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:43.080
Lien was arrested in 2015 for indecent exposure&nbsp;
and again in 2018 for driving without a license.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.840
Actor Robert Picardo, already known for&nbsp;
antagonistic roles in Gremlins 2 and InnerSpace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.820
starred on Voyager as the Doctor, the nameless&nbsp;
holographic chief medical officer whose program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.800
was left running far longer than it was designed&nbsp;
to. Known for his offbeat humor and cantankerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.840
attitude, he was played to perfection by Picardo,&nbsp;
and it would become the actor's signature role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.440
though his film history as weird&nbsp;
aliens and monsters is iconic as well.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:15.060
"I watched four episodes of Lassie before I&nbsp;
figured out why the little hairy kid never spoke!"

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.740
Still, even after leaving sickbay as the&nbsp;
Doctor, Picardo had a healthy career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:25.440
moving quickly into a role in The Lyon's Den&nbsp;
in 2003. A year later he joined another iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.000
sci-fi franchise when he secured a recurring&nbsp;
part in Stargate SG-1 as Richard Woolsey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.820
a grumpy U.S. official who&nbsp;
opposed the Stargate program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.340
Following a string of appearances on the flagship&nbsp;
series, Picardo joined the main cast of Stargate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:44.580
Atlantis in 2006. A few years later, Picardo had&nbsp;
another repeat role, this time as Jason Cooper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:48.780
on The Mentalist, and he later enjoyed a&nbsp;
stint on the Apple TV+ drama Dickinson.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:54.000
In 2023, the actor made a guest appearance on&nbsp;
the Quantum Leap revival playing Doctor Woolsey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.820
whose name is a clear tribute&nbsp;
to his two biggest TV roles.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.100
Late in Season 6 of Voyager, a storyline saw&nbsp;
the ship rescue a stranded vessel commanded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:05.340
a group of wayward Borg children.&nbsp;
At the conclusion of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:09.420
four young drones join the crew, becoming&nbsp;
a surrogate family of sorts to Seven of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.560
Nine. The eldest of them is Icheb, a&nbsp;
teenager played by actor Manu Intiraymi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:17.340
who went on to make 11 appearances&nbsp;
across the final two seasons of the show.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:21.060
When Voyager ended in 2001,&nbsp;
Intiraymi continued acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:25.140
with his largest role coming in One Tree Hill,&nbsp;
where he played Billy, a local drug dealer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:30.600
in a recurring role in 2012. Further projects&nbsp;
were mostly independent films like 5th Passenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.940
in 2017 alongside a number of other Star Trek&nbsp;
stars, and the 2019 western Hell on the Border.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:41.580
In 2017, Intiraymi came under fire for&nbsp;
criticizing fellow Star Trek actor Anthony Rapp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:46.020
who'd made accusations of sexual assault&nbsp;
against Kevin Spacey. A few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.160
fans speculated those comments may have been&nbsp;
why he wasn't asked to return to the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.700
Icheb in Picard, with a new actor playing the&nbsp;
part in a scene that killed off the character.

